<Project>
  <!--
    The AOT workaround, kept HERE rather than in every app that publishes with -p:Aot=true.

    Mono's WASM AOT mis-compiles a call inside CupriFace method bodies, and the app dies at boot
    with `RuntimeError: null function or function signature mismatch`. Keeping CupriFace
    interpreted (the jiterpreter still applies) costs roughly 8x native on the measured showcase —
    hover restyle 16.2 ms/move, section switch 60.7 ms at 940x720 — while CoreLib, AngleSharp,
    Regex and the Skia bindings all stay AOT.

    WHAT THE FAULT ACTUALLY IS (re-investigated on SDK 10.0.302, 2026-08). It is not one bad
    method. It is whichever AngleSharp INTERFACE call runs first from an AOT-compiled CupriFace
    body, and the earlier note here — which named SliderComponent.Expand — was describing the
    symptom's location on that day rather than its cause. Three measurements say so:

      * With the workaround off, the trap symbolises to CupriDocument.ApplyInputProfile, via
        Rebuild → Load → CreateDocument. NOT SliderComponent.Expand.
      * Rewriting ApplyInputProfile to avoid AngleSharp's ITokenList entirely (ClassName string
        manipulation instead of ClassList.Add/Remove) fixes THAT site — and the trap moves
        straight to SliderComponent.Expand's first `el.GetAttribute(name)`, which is where the
        original investigation found it. Fixing call sites is whack-a-mole: the engine has ~419
        AngleSharp interface call sites across 43 files.
      * Force-interpreting AngleSharp.dll INSTEAD of CupriFace.dll does not help. The bad code is
        in the AOT body of the CALLER, so interpreting the callee changes nothing.

    So this cannot be fixed from our side by changing code, and narrowing the interpret set to
    something smaller than CupriFace.dll needs the DOM-facing half of the engine to be separable
    from the layout/paint half — which it currently is not, because a hover restyle re-runs the
    style resolver over the DOM. It is an upstream Mono codegen bug; ours to retest, not to fix.

    TO RETEST after an SDK bump (about five minutes):
      dotnet publish samples/WebWasm/WebWasm.csproj -c Release -p:Aot=true -p:CupriInterpretAssemblies=" "
      then serve bin/Release/net10.0/publish/wwwroot and open it — a white canvas with
      "signature mismatch" in the page's #bootlog means it is still broken.
    To symbolise the wasm-function[N] frames, map them through
      obj/Release/net10.0/wasm/for-publish/dotnet.native.js.symbols
    (WasmEmitSymbolMap is already on for AOT publishes). If it boots and paints, delete this file
    and the property below, and take the 8x back.

    Why it belongs to the package: it is a workaround for a defect that bites CupriFace's own code,
    and it was previously carried in consumers' csproj files, where nobody could tell them when it
    stopped being necessary.

    Applied only when the app actually AOT-compiles, so a plain build or `dotnet run` is untouched.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(RunAOTCompilation)' == 'true'">
    <!-- CupriInterpretAssemblies overrides the set, for the retest recipe above. A single space
         means "interpret nothing", which is how the fault is reproduced. -->
    <_AOT_InternalForceInterpretAssemblies Include="$(CupriInterpretAssemblies)" Condition="'$(CupriInterpretAssemblies)' != ''" />
    <_AOT_InternalForceInterpretAssemblies Include="CupriFace.dll" Condition="'$(CupriInterpretAssemblies)' == ''" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
